# Build context for the Dockerfiles built with context `.` that have no ignore file of
# their own: infra/docker/Dockerfile (mm-core) and infra/docker/Dockerfile.fakestripe.
# (Dockerfile.web uses infra/docker/Dockerfile.web.dockerignore, which BuildKit reads in
# preference to this file; mm-switch's context is services/mm-switch.)
#
# ALLOWLIST: exclude everything, then re-include only what the Rust build reads. The old
# denylist let every new non-Rust path in (.github/, deploy/, scripts/, terraform/, …), and
# since those Dockerfiles did `COPY . .`, a workflow-only PR forced a full release compile.
# The Dockerfiles now COPY these paths explicitly, so this file mainly keeps the context
# small; if a Dockerfile using context `.` fails with "not found", re-include the path here.
*
!Cargo.toml
!Cargo.lock
!crates
# crates/mm-api include_str!s mm-core/data/reserved_usernames.json.
!mm-core/data

# Local build output and OS junk inside the re-included trees.
**/target
**/.DS_Store

# rust-toolchain.toml is deliberately NOT re-included. The toolchain pin is a dev/CI concern,
# and it must NOT reach the image builder. infra/docker/Dockerfile builds
# `FROM rust:1-slim-bookworm` (floating latest stable) and the official image ships rustup — so
# a rust-toolchain.toml in the build context would make the first `cargo` invocation
# rustup-fetch 1.93.1 plus rustfmt and clippy (which an image build has no use for) on EVERY
# build, and would break every production image build outright if that exact version ever
# became unavailable. The cleaner fix — pin the base image to rust:1.93.1-slim-bookworm so the
# toolchain file is a no-op there and dev/CI/image all agree — is a Dockerfile change and is
# deliberately out of scope here.
